(7665080, 1250057, 4008, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by tnun:</b></div><hr/><i> Oh that Willem, no spring for he. Possible he doesn''t know what the H stands for. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>I do, but still the image does not work for me. I looked at the image, not the title. I realised this was about the pandemic and this is currently happening in springtime, but still, for me it does not connect to the challenge theme. <br/> <br/>As to the technical aspects, an image does not always have to be eye candy or technically perfect for me, but in my view a different technical approach should add to the message one wants to give with the image. I did not get that here.<br/> <br/>I hope this add some further nuance to my explanation and score (2). At least I bothered to explain my score, which many others did not.</td>', 'Originally posted by tnun: Oh that Willem, no spring for he. Possible he doesn''t know what the H stands for.   I do, but still the image does not work for me. I looked at the image, not the title. I realised this was about the pandemic and this is currently happening in springtime, but still, for me it does not connect to the challenge theme.  As to the technical aspects, an image does not always have to be eye candy or technically perfect for me, but in my view a different technical approach should add to the message one wants to give with the image. I did not get that here. I hope this add some further nuance to my explanation and score (2). At least I bothered to explain my score, which many others did not.', '2020-06-03 12:50:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(7665082, 1249454, 4008, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Lydia:</b></div><hr/><i> Congrats on your BLUE, Willem!<br/> <br/>Awful news about the babies.  Do magpies do that a lot? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Yes, they do. Magpies are very smart, they often work together as a group and they eat anything they can get their hands on, young birds, mice, frogs, etc. They even came into our house (just a few feet) to steal cat food. One bird entered, while the others stood on the lookout. They also love to harass other animals (for example our rabbit) as a group, like local mobsters. Very funny to see.</td>', 'Originally posted by Lydia: Congrats on your BLUE, Willem! Awful news about the babies.  Do magpies do that a lot?   Yes, they do. Magpies are very smart, they often work together as a group and they eat anything they can get their hands on, young birds, mice, frogs, etc. They even came into our house (just a few feet) to steal cat food. One bird entered, while the others stood on the lookout. They also love to harass other animals (for example our rabbit) as a group, like local mobsters. Very funny to see.', '2020-06-03 13:04:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(7665084, 1249656, 52549, '<td valign="top">This is soo pretty, Barbara. Herz allerliebst. Congrats on TT.</td>', 'This is soo pretty, Barbara. Herz allerliebst. Congrats on TT.', '2020-06-03 13:19:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(7665118, 1250061, 99263, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by vawendy:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by ThingFish:</b></div><hr/><i> They look exactly like our Cape Gannets here in South Africa which are not found anywhere else in the world except Africa..well according to this article with photos about them. <br/><a href="https://www.sa-venues.com/wildlife/birds_cape_gannets.php" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Cape Gannets</a> <br/> <br/>What is the sppecies that you photographed called and where did you photograph these? </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>These are northern gannets photographed at <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bempton_Cliffs" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Bempton Cliffs</a> with <img alt="'' . substr(''https://www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/31_F.gif'', strrpos(''https://www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/31_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="https://www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/31_F.gif"/> <a href="https://www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=67258" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">sarar</a> and <img alt="'' . substr(''https://www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', strrpos(''https://www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="https://www.dpchallenge.com/images/user_icon/21_F.gif"/> <a href="https://www.dpchallenge.com/profile.php?USER_ID=149964" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">pangurban</a>.<br/> <br/>h </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>That''s interesting. They look so alike. I found this article which mentions that similarity also. "The Cape gannet is identified from the Australasian gannet by the all-black tail, a longer black stripe down the throat (from underneath the beak) and more extensive black on the face, but all of these features are difficult to identify unless at close proximity. The Cape gannet is also very similar to the northern gannet, but the latter differs from its entirely white tail and its wings, which are only black at the tip. It also is similar to the blue-footed booby (Sula nebouxii)."<br/><a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_gannet" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_gannet</a></td>', 'Originally posted by vawendy: Originally posted by ThingFish: They look exactly like our Cape Gannets here in South Africa which are not found anywhere else in the world except Africa..well according to this article with photos about them. Cape Gannets  What is the sppecies that you photographed called and where did you photograph these?   These are northern gannets photographed at Bempton Cliffs with  sarar and  pangurban. h   That''s interesting. They look so alike. I found this article which mentions that similarity also. "The Cape gannet is identified from the Australasian gannet by the all-black tail, a longer black stripe down the throat (from underneath the beak) and more extensive black on the face, but all of these features are difficult to identify unless at close proximity. The Cape gannet is also very similar to the northern gannet, but the latter differs from its entirely white tail and its wings, which are only black at the tip. It also is similar to the blue-footed booby (Sula nebouxii)."https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cape_gannet', '2020-06-03 19:43:31.000000', NULL, 0),
